s-angeles-times-book-prize-for-current-interest"},"statusLabel":"Finalist"}],"imprint":"Spiegel & Grau","publisher":"Penguin Random House","stats":{"bookId":"book-just-mercy-a-story-of-justice-and-redemption-bryan-stevenson","wins":2,"lists":4,"score":22,"majorWins":1,"normalWins":1,"majorShortlists":2,"normalShortlists":0,"majorLonglists":0,"normalLonglists":0,"statuses":{"winner":2,"co_winner":0,"finalist":2,"shortlist":0,"longlist":0,"honorable_mention":0,"commended":0,"notable":0,"unknown":0}},"wikipediaEvidence":{"bookId":"book-just-mercy-a-story-of-justice-and-redemption-bryan-stevenson","pageTitle":"Just Mercy (book)","pageId":66124366,"wikidataId":"Q45349891","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_Mercy_(book)","revisionId":1351430297,"accessedAt":"2026-05-18T21:16:03.977Z","extract":"Just Mercy: A Story of Justice and Redemption (2014) is a memoir by American attorney Bryan Stevenson that documents his career defending disadvantaged clients. The book, focusing on injustices in the United States judicial system, alternates chapters between documenting Stevenson's efforts to overturn the wrongful conviction of Walter McMillian and his work on other cases, including children who receive life sentences, and other poor or marginalized clients. The memoir has received many honors and won multiple non-fiction book awards. It was a New York Times best seller and spent more than 230 weeks on the paperback nonfiction best sellers list. It won the 2015 Andrew Carnegie Medal for Excellence in Nonfiction, given annually by the American Library Association. The book was also awarded the 2015 Dayton Literary Peace Prize for Nonfiction and the 2015 NAACP Image Award for Outstanding Literary Work in Nonfiction. It was named one of \"10 of the decade's most influential books\" in December 2019 by CNN. Published in hardcover and digital formats in 2014 by Spiegel & Grau, then an imprint of Penguin Random House, it was released by Random House Audio in audiobook format read by Stevenson. It has also been published in paperback. A young adult adaptation was published by Delacorte Press on September 18, 2018. The memoir was adapted as a 2019 movie of the same name by Destin Daniel Cretton. To commemorate the film, \"Movie Tie-In\" editions were released for both versions of the memoir on December 3, 2019 by imprints of Penguin Random House.","matchedBy":"infobox_book:high","confidence":"high","attribution":{"label":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_Mercy_(book)","license":"CC BY-SA","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"},"infobox":{"title":"Just Mercy","author":"Bryan Stevenson","publisher":"Spiegel & Grau","publicationDate":"October 21, 2014","pages":"336","isbn":"978-0-8129-9452-0"},"publisherEvidenceId":"publisher-evidence-wikipedia-just-mercy-a-story-of-justice-and-redemption-bryan-stevenson"},"sources":[{"id":"source-award-record-carnegie-nonfiction-wikipedia-andrew-carnegie-medals-for-excellence-in-fiction-and-nonfiction","label":"Wikipedia: Andrew Carnegie Medals for Excellence in Fiction and Nonfiction","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Carnegie_Medals_for_Excellence_in_Fiction_and_Nonfiction","accessedAt":"2026-08-20T21:35:58.016Z","confidence":"secondary","field":"award","note":"Official awards URL: https://www.ala.org/rusa/awards/carnegie-medals"},{"id":"source-award-record-dayton-nonfiction-wikipedia-dayton-literary-peace-prize-nonfiction-table","label":"Wikipedia: Dayton Literary Peace Prize nonfiction table","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayton_Literary_Peace_Prize","accessedAt":"2026-08-20T21:35:58.016Z","confidence":"secondary","field":"award","note":"Official awards URL: https://www.daytonliterarypeaceprize.org/"},{"id":"source-award-record-kirkus-nonfiction-wikipedia-kirkus-prize","label":"Wikipedia: Kirkus Prize","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirkus_Prize","accessedAt":"2026-08-20T21:35:58.016Z","confidence":"secondary","field":"award","note":"Offici